# Feature-Sliced Design

When creating/moving modules, choosing layers, public APIs, or cross-module imports.

## Source of truth

1. Follow Feature-Sliced Design: `https://fsd.how/`.
2. Before non-trivial structure decisions (new slice/layer, cross-import, public API shape): open the relevant FSD docs pages — do not invent a parallel architecture.
3. Cite concrete `fsd.how` URLs when recommending structure or import rules.
4. Enforcement config lives in `@steiger.config.mjs` (`@feature-sliced/steiger-plugin` recommended). Treat Steiger failures as architecture bugs to fix, not to silence casually.

## Where code lives

- Application feature code belongs under `src` (Steiger is run on `./src`).
- Root RN/web entry files (`App.tsx`, `index.js`, `index.web.js`, native folders, `web/`) may stay outside slices as bootstrap — keep business UI and domain logic in FSD layers under `src`.

## Core preferences (do not restate the whole handbook)

- Respect FSD layers and import direction: higher layers may import lower; lower must not import higher.
- Organize by slices/segments per docs; expose a clear public API (`index`) instead of deep cross-slice imports.
- Prefer extending existing slices over dumping unrelated code into `shared` or a catch-all folder.
- If FSD docs and memory conflict, the official docs win.
